{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779395933,"nonce":"cp8zHYbW1tgietbLr1B0LZe2JQ8=","title":"Studio compatibility UX for ports, bindings, and conflicts","message":"Parent: 0258fac\nDepends on: 974b9ba, 0866f1d\n\n## Summary\nBuild the Studio compatibility UX for port schemas, Task Data bindings, inferred parameter updates, and conflicts. This is the user-facing slice of schema-driven dimension propagation.\n\n## UX principles\n- Keep the canvas interaction-first. Do not add always-visible schema tables to every node.\n- Use compact port affordances, contextual tooltips, selected-port inspector details, and grouped validation rows.\n- Show the reason and source of a compatibility decision at the moment it matters: connect hover/drop, selected port, selected binding, selected synced param, and validation panel.\n- Unknown schema is a warning state, not a hard error. Conflicting known constraints are hard errors.\n\n## Port and binding display\n- Port handles remain small, but their color/shape should be schema-driven enough to separate vector, scalar, state, any/unknown, and task-bound ports.\n- Hovering or selecting a port shows a compact schema summary: label, direction, semantic type, element dtype, shape/rank, units/frame when known, and provenance.\n- Task Data source hints should show the Task Data label plus schema summary in tooltip, e.g. `Target position · float32 [time, 2] -\u003e sample [2]`.\n- Synced params such as `Network.input_size` and `Network.out_size` show a subtle linked state and tooltip/source line, e.g. `Synced from task_mux.output = 4` or `Synced from mechanics.force = 2`.\n\n## Connect-time behavior\n- Compatible edge: normal connect affordance.\n- Compatible edge with inferred update: connect affordance should say what will change, e.g. `Set network.out_size to 6`.\n- Unknown schema: allow if structurally valid, but mark as warning and show `Compatibility unknown`.\n- Conflict: block the connection or leave the graph invalid according to the current store policy, but show one concise reason naming both sides and the conflicting param/source. Avoid generic \"type mismatch\" when the real problem is `Network.out_size` or `Network.input_size`.\n\n## Validation/inspector behavior\n- Validation panel groups compatibility issues by surface: Graph wires, Task Data bindings, Synced dimensions, Unknown schemas.\n- Selecting an issue highlights the relevant port/edge/binding and, when applicable, the affected param row.\n- Selected graph port and Task Data entities in the Scenario inspector should display schema and compatibility status without requiring the user to open raw JSON.\n- Existing task-source emerald hints and objective/probe violet affordances should remain recognizable; schema colors should not collide with those meanings.\n\n## Copy conventions\nUse concrete nouns and values:\n- Good: `network.output has shape [2], but muscle.excitation expects [6]`.\n- Good: `Network.out_size is 2, inferred 6 from Arm6MuscleRigidTendon.excitation`.\n- Good: `Target position is a trajectory; binding uses per-step sample shape [2]`.\n- Avoid: `Incompatible schema`, `invalid type`, or raw JSON-only explanations.\n\n## Out of scope\n- A full standalone schema browser.\n- Generic value/function/distribution editor work under `bd5aefb`.\n- Workspace spatial authoring under `946c859`.\n\n## Done criteria\n- Port hover/selection, connection feedback, synced param display, and validation grouping are implemented and covered by focused frontend tests where practical.\n- Browser verification covers the default simple reaching graph, the delayed reach mux binding path, a mechanics output-size sync, an unknown-schema warning, and a conflict.\n- UI text fits within existing panels/nodes at desktop and narrow widths; schema detail lives in tooltips/inspectors, not persistent canvas clutter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}